.282 ± 0.006 | 0.180 | 0.10 |","+   | 2 | (5, ½) | 2.06 | 0.232 ± 0.005 | 0.169 | 0.06 |","+   | 2 | (3, 1) | 2.04 | 0.200 ± 0.003 | 0.147 | 0.05 |","+   | 2 | (2, 2) | 2.02 | 0.167 ± 0.002 | 0.123 | 0.04 |","+   | 3 | (7, ½) | 3.01 | 0.400 ± 0.005 | 0.327 | 0.07 |","+   | 3 | (4, 1) | 3.02 | 0.348 ± 0.003 | 0.288 | 0.06 |","+   | 3 | (12, ¼) | 2.73 (outside ±0.05) | 0.405 ± 0.006 | 0.306 | 0.10 |","+   | 4 | (9, ½) | 3.99 | 0.517 ± 0.011 | 0.463 | 0.05 |","+   | 4 | (5, 1) | 4.00 | 0.479 ± 0.003 | 0.411 | 0.07 |","+   | 4 | (3, 2) | 4.00 | 0.409 ± 0.003 | 0.356 | 0.05 |","+   | 6 | (7, 1) | 6.00 | 0.651 ± 0.003 | 0.595 | 0.06 |","+   | 6 | (4, 2) | 6.00 | 0.568 ± 0.003 | 0.525 | 0.04 |","+   | 8 | (9, 1) | 8.00 | 0.752 ± 0.002 | 0.714 | 0.04 |","+   | 8 | (5, 2) | 8.00 | 0.685 ± 0.002 | 0.642 | 0.04 |","+","+   (`±` is one standard error.) At every excess, **the slope falls with `μ`","+   at fixed `e`**: at `e = 2` from 0.282 (few, large patterns, `μ = ¼`) to","+   0.167 (many small ones, `μ = 2`), a spread of 0.115 against a standard","+   error of 0.006 — twenty times the noise. The three pairs that do not","+   differ are `(4, ¼)` against `(2, 1)` at `e ≈ 1.1` (both near the","+   threshold, slopes 0.03–0.04), `(7, ¼)` against `(4, ½)` at `e ≈ 1.62`, and","+   the fixed `(10, 1)` series against the Chu & Stuckey `d = 10` class, which","+   is the same model with padding. `e` sets the scale of `opt / n`","+   (correlation of `b` with `log e` 0.988 over the 41 constant-`e` series) and","+   `μ` moves it by up to 0.1 at fixed `e`; so **`lim opt / n`, if it exists,","+   is a function of `(d, μ)` and not of `e` alone.**","+","+3. **The first moment has the two-parameter dependence in the right","+   direction.** `β*(d, μ)` falls with `μ` at fixed `e` exactly as `b` does","+   (at `e = 2`: 0.180, 0.169, 0.147, 0.123 against 0.282, 0.232, 0.200,","+   0.167), and over the 41 constant-`e` series `b = 0.057 + 0.984 β*` with","+   residual standard deviation **0.024** (0.015 with a quadratic), against","+   0.042 for the best line in `log e` (0.033 for a cubic). The slack","+   `b − β*` is 0.005–0.108, median 0.053, and **peaks on the ridge**: mean","+   0.026 at `e ≤ 1.5`, 0.069–0.071 at `e = 1.5–4`, 0.036 at `e > 6`, largest","+   on the `μ = ¼` series at `e = 1.8–2.7` (0.09–0.11). `β*` is not the","+   constant either: of 30 pairs matched in `β*` to ±0.02, 14 differ in `b`.","+   But the first moment is a far better one-parameter coordinate for","+   `opt / n` than the excess is, which is what item 08's structured count has","+   to improve on and item 09's ridge hypothesis stands on.","+","+4. **The rule.** Over all 57,077 certified instances the bare two-key rule","+   (no DFS behind it) is exact on 83.3% of the corpus and 62.2% of the","+   ensembles; on the 200 Chu & Stuckey instances it is exact on 27%, mean","+   gap 2.35, worst 10 (the `125 × 125` `d = 2` and `d = 4` classes, mean","+   gaps 6.8). **Its gap peaks on the ridge** in every source: on the","+   ensembles the exact share falls from 98% at `e < 0.62` to 22% at","+   `e ∈ [1.5, 2)` and 27% at `[2.4, 3)`, then climbs to 91% at `e ≥ 10`; the","+   mean gap per customer is 0.03–0.04 at `e = 1.5–3` and below 0.002 at the","+   ends. The worst gap is 14 (`n = 100`, `m = 50`, `e = 2.5`, optimum 37,","+   rule 51), and every one of the ten worst cases is at `n = 100` with","+   `m ≤ n / 2`. Per constant-`e` series, the rule's `width / n` at the largest","+   `n` sits 0.04–0.15 above `b` (median 0.08), by more at small `μ`; and on","+   the ridge the rule's excess per customer grows with `n` (0.03 at `n = 30`,","+   0.046 at 75, 0.058 at 100), so the rule's `width / n` is not an estimate","+   of the slope at the largest sizes, and item 05 should expect it to drift","+   upward at `n = 1,000–5,000`.","+","+5. **A stale table, corrected in passing.** `learning/data/instances.csv`","+   (2026-09-25) carries `provenance = solution` for four `125 × 125`","+   instances certified in October 2026 (`Random-125-125-2-2_0`, `2-3_0`,","+   `2-5_0`, `4-4_0`); item 01's build took its corpus optima from it, so","+   `learning/data/excess.csv` had 6,372 certified corpus rows. `corpus_rows`","+   now reads the value and provenance from the solution file (read only), the","+   map is rebuilt with 6,376 of 6,376 certified, and no number in §1 changes","+   (none of its tables uses the optimum). The four values are right in","+   `solutions/`; nothing was re-solved.","+","+### 2.2 Kill verdict","+","+**Met: the hypothesis \"a function of `e` alone\" is dead.** 13 of the 16","+constant-`e` matched pairs (26 of all 30) have slopes that differ by more","+than twice the larger standard error; the dependence on `μ` at fixed `e` is","+monotone, twenty standard errors wide at `e = 2`, and the first moment has","+it in the same direction. The finding recorded for items 05, 07, 08 and 09:","+**the parameter of the constant is `(d, μ)`, or, to a residual of 0.024,","+`β*(d, μ)`; the excess sets the threshold, the ridge and the scale, not the","+value.**","+","+### 2.3 What this does not claim","+","+- **A limit.** `b` is a finite-range slope on `n = 30–100` (30–125 for the","+  Chu & Stuckey classes). `opt / n` at the largest `n` is above `b` on every","+  series (the intercepts `a` are 3–9), `pw / n` falls with `n` at every fixed","+  density to 75, and §14 showed that constants fitted at `n ≤ 40` fail at","+  125. Whether the two slopes of a differing pair converge as `n → ∞` is not","+  tested here; what is shown is that at `n = 30–100` they differ by twenty","+  standard errors, and that the first-moment constants, which are limits,","+  differ in the same direction.","+- **That `β*` is the constant.** `b = 0.057 + 0.984 β*` is a description of","+  41 series, not a theorem; 14 of 30 `β*`-matched pairs still differ, and the","+  slack peaks on the ridge where the solver also struggles.","+- **Anything about the Bernoulli generator at fixed `p`.** Its series are not","+  constant-excess series and were excluded from the kill count; its 31 fits","+  are tabulated for completeness only.","+- **That the rule is a bound.** It is a heuristic, exact on 83% of the corpus","+  and worst on the ridge; `rule / n` is an upper envelope that drifts up with","+  `n` there.","+","+### 2.4 Figure","+","+`reports/figures/excess_opt.png`: one panel per product ratio `r = m / n`,","+the 41 constant-`e` series only (circles the fixed generator at `n = 30–100`,","+squares the Chu & Stuckey classes at `n = 30–125`); against the realised `e`,","+the certified slope `b` with its ±2 se bar (the bars are smaller than the","+markers), the rule's `width / n` at the largest `n`, and `β*` at the realised","+sizes. The grey line is `e = 1`, the band `e = 2–2.4`. Three quantities on","+three categorical slots; the Bernoulli series are left out for the reason in","+finding 1.","+","+![opt / n against the excess](figures/excess_opt.png)","+","+### 2.5 Tables, in full","+","+Scored: 57077 certified instances (corpus 6376, ensemble 50701), rule time 30.1 s in all; series with >= 3 sizes at n >= 30: 72 (41 with e constant in n to within 10% of its mean; the Bernoulli series at fixed p have e growing with n and are not constant-e series). e-matched pairs (|Δe| <= 0.05, different (generator, d or p, mu)): 30, of which 26 differ by more than twice the larger standard error; among the 16 pairs of two constant-e series, 13 differ. Verdict: the one-parameter hypothesis is DEAD (slopes differ on at least half of the constant-e matched pairs).","+","+","+### Per series: opt = a + b n on n >= 30, the rule, beta*","+","+| generator   | r   |   param |      e |   e range over n |    mu |   mean size | n sizes                     |   instances |        a |       b |     se b |   b rule |   opt/n at max n |   rule/n at max n |   beta* (sizes) |   beta* (fixed d) |   rule exact |   rule gap mean |   rule gap max |","+|:------------|:----|--------:|-------:|--------